# Scoped review: fixed untimed Labs warmup (22 October 2026)

**Source review outcome.** The seven-file isolated diff is clear for adoption into the held checkout, followed by a pinned pnpm patch rehash/install and deterministic gates before any timing. This is source clearance, not a passing gate or a performance admission. No benchmark, build, package install, or protocol test was run for this review. `git diff --check` passed.

The explicit `--sampling single-call --warmup fixed-20` path reaches Labs config, CLI, isolated worker, and measurement options. In `benchFn`, the original first call still supplies `$first` and the generator result; exactly 20 additional calls occur before the generated timed loop, independent of the 0.5 ms warmup threshold and the early batch break. Warmup parameter providers, consumption, `after`, and async completion remain on the original call path. The patch does not alter the timer, forced GC, trim, sample budget, or pilot/replay scheduling. The default `conditional` policy retains its old loop and break behavior.

**Iterator handoff checked explicitly.** Pinned `benchIter` has an extra callback at its existing generator-to-timed-loop handoff. The fixed branch now makes 19 loop calls plus that handoff after the original first call, for 21 untimed callbacks total. Its plan counts the handoff. The direct protocol probe asserts 21 callbacks at the first timed clock read for function, generator, and iterator paths, and checks the historical conditional iterator count of three under the fast-control fixture. Repository package benchmarks are generator fixtures routed by `benchGenerator` through `benchFn`; the iterator correction keeps the patched low-level API coherent without changing the old iterator handoff.

The worker records `plan.warmup_calls` and per-block `blocks.warmupCalls`; the package runner requires 21 in the pilot and each of eight fresh blocks when fixed-20 is requested. The manifest records the requested policy. Saved candidate validation rejects a requested policy mismatch before baseline timing and checks observed counts for fixed-20; a missing policy field is interpreted only as the historical conditional policy. The deterministic protocol source also checks first-result preservation, parameter/consume/after counts, worker propagation, and invalid combinations. These are assertions present in source; their execution remains a later gate.

**Fixture postconditions.** Cold mount, edit, and borrow callbacks construct and dispose fresh state per invocation. Typing, reorder, color/text, and prepared-operation workloads alternate repeatable states; adding an even 20 calls after the first preserves the parity at the start of sampling for those two-state callbacks. The stress visibility fixture restores visibility before its postcondition. Prepared-operation checks use the first result for the oracle and derive final expected text from the actual last invocation. This was a source audit of representative stateful cases, not execution of all 145 identities; the complete fixed-family run must still retain all correctness failures and adverse tails.

**Adoption prerequisite.** The isolated `pnpm-lock.yaml` still names the previous Labs patch hash (`1475111a563f675ba8de804b57a4a7500acad4f500771713dad8ad33b5be3002`). It must be refreshed through the pinned pnpm workflow when the seven files are adopted, followed by protocol, static, unit, and workflow gates. Do not compare new fixed-20 results with old conditional saved candidate distributions. The original 113 ms 512-label block did not recur in the bounded diagnostic, so this policy change cannot be described as a demonstrated spike fix. No new matrix should begin until the broader benchmark input tree is stable.

